<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Trimester, I Am Learning About... – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Trimester, I Am Learning About...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a short report on a class activity. Interactive: Edit classmates' narratives for clarity and coherence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short report on a class learning activity (e.g., "Today, we are discussing famous leaders. First, we read about their achievements. Then, we answered questions."). Can write a summary of the main points learned in a history lesson (e.g., "Three main points are: Panama's independence, the construction of the Panama Canal, and indigenous peoples and cultures."). Can edit a peer's short narrative for clarity (e.g., add, "in 2025" to make the date clear).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to think about the current trimester and what subjects or activities they are actively learning about. Students brainstorm on mini-whiteboards or in notebooks, listing 2-3 things. Example prompt: "What subjects or skills are you developing this trimester?"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models by writing a short report on the board about a class activity they experienced recently. For instance: "Last week, we discussed famous leaders. Today, I am learning about effective lesson planning. First, I read about the AOA stages. Then, I am preparing activities for a writing lesson. I am interested in helping students achieve their learning outcomes. I would like to see them grow their writing skills." While writing, the teacher highlights (e.g., by underlining) the present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, and modals used. The teacher also emphasizes the stress patterns in "I *am* learning" and "I *would* like."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I talking about something that happened in the past or something happening now?" (Expected answer: "Now.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I am interested in *learning* English,' is 'learning' a verb or a noun here?" (Expected answer: "It acts like a noun, a gerund.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides a handout or displays on the projector a short model report of a fictional 8th-grade history class activity, structured with sequential events. The text will incorporate the target grammar (present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, modals) and vocabulary (e.g., *education, skills, knowledge, subject, trimester, history, classmate, learn, study, develop, grow, achieve, educational, critical, academic, motivated, ambitious, curious, actively, well*).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b> "Our History Class Activity: This Trimester, we are actively learning about Panama's history. Last week, we discussed the construction of the Panama Canal. Today, we are studying the indigenous peoples and cultures. First, we read an educational text about their traditions. Then, we are preparing a presentation. I am interested in developing my critical thinking skills through this subject. My classmate, Ana, is also learning a lot. We would like to achieve good grades. She is really motivated."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students recognize and understand the structure and linguistic features of a short report describing a class activity or learning experience, focusing on sequence and present continuous actions. They practice identifying how to discuss learning goals and progress.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the report silently.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main subject of this report?" (Expected answer: Panama's history and indigenous cultures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The students are studying math today." (False). "They are interested in developing critical thinking skills." (True).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify sentences using the present continuous and gerunds after prepositions in the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using the present continuous for ongoing actions, gerunds after prepositions, and modals for ability and desires, incorporating target vocabulary related to education and learning.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill:</b> Students complete sentences with the correct form of the verb (present continuous or gerund) or an appropriate modal from a word bank. Example sentences: "I am interested in _____ (learn) about space." "My classmates _____ (study) for the history test right now." "We _____ like to visit a university next year." (Vocabulary: learn, study, interested in, actively, develop, grow, achieve, academic, motivated). The teacher reviews answers orally, paying attention to stress patterns in modals ("*would* like") and continuous forms ("*am* learning").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Sentence Transformation:</b> Students rewrite simple sentences to include a gerund after a preposition or a modal. Example: "I want to improve my skills." -> "I would like to improve my skills." or "I enjoy science." -> "I am interested in studying science." This activity also includes word choices to practice minimal pair sounds, e.g., using "skill" and "head" vs. "had" in context to refine writing choices.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Writing & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes 3-4 sentences describing something they are *currently learning* or *planning to learn* this trimester, using at least one present continuous, one gerund, and one modal. They use vocabulary like *subject, course, knowledge, career goals*. They then exchange sentences with their partner and check for correct grammar and clear meaning. They offer encouragement: "You should keep practicing to get better."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Write a Class Activity Report & Peer Edit.</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will individually write a short report (4-6 sentences) on a specific class activity they participated in recently or a summary of main points learned in a particular subject *this trimester*. They should use the model report from Stage 2 as a reference.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must include: at least two sentences in the present continuous, one sentence with a gerund after a preposition, and one sentence with a modal (e.g., "I would like to..."). They are encouraged to use at least three vocabulary words from the list (e.g., *education, skills, knowledge, subject, trimester, learn, develop, motivated*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, students exchange their reports with a new partner. Each student reads their partner's report and provides feedback on clarity and coherence, as well as checking for the correct use of target grammar and vocabulary. They can suggest adding details (e.g., "add, 'in 2025' to make the date clear") or rephrasing for better flow. This activity promotes engaging in discussions about personal and learning goals and offering relevant suggestions for improvement, respecting others' opinions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during peer editing, collection of revised reports, and a simple peer assessment checklist.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students finalize their written reports based on peer feedback. They then submit their reports along with the peer feedback comments. The teacher collects the reports to review for understanding and application of the learning outcomes.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the report (easy to understand the class activity/summary).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present continuous for ongoing actions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of gerunds after prepositions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals (e.g., "I would like to...").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion and correct use of at least three target vocabulary words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall grammar and spelling (at A2.3 level).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about writing today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one part of your report you are proud of, and one part you would like to improve?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did giving and receiving feedback help you make your report clearer and more coherent?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies will you use next time you write a report about a class activity or summarise learning points?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., peer editing was effective, students used target grammar accurately).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further develop their writing skills, especially concerning coherence or specific grammar points? (e.g., provide more sentence starters, focus on transition words).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on writing and editing individual reports. In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their reports and summaries to consolidate information and present their learning to the class, possibly integrating it into a larger project about their trimester achievements, which will involve oral mediation and summarization skills.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-4 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>